<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage a Club</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate discussions on project goals and tasks. Text: Clarify written instructions in collaborative projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate group discussions by encouraging participation, clarifying ideas, and summarizing goals (e.g., "Let's brainstorm ideas for improving the club activities." "So far, we've agreed to organize a school clean-up day and a community reading event."). Can clarify written instructions for organizing a club activity by providing a visual checklist (e.g., drawing steps on the board: "Step 1: Find members; Step 2: Choose activities"). Can help group members understand club tasks by rephrasing instructions (e.g., "Let's set up a meeting every week," while pointing to a calendar).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a visual of a "Club Promotion Video" on the board (e.g., a storyboard or a screenshot). Students work in pairs to brainstorm what steps are needed to create such a video, recalling previous discussions on club management. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What do we need to *organize* first?" or "Who will *manage* different *responsibilities* for the video *project*?" (Recalls vocabulary: organize, manage, responsibilities, project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify a task description. For example, the teacher writes "Make the video" on the board, then says, "This instruction is a bit vague. How can we *clarify* it? We need to *delegate* tasks. Let's *plan* who will do what." The teacher then rephrases: "First, *assign* roles: Who will film? Who will write the script? Who will *coordinate*?" (Models verbs: clarify, delegate, plan, assign, coordinate, and emphasizes stress on -ate verbs).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone says 'Let's just do it,' and you don't understand, what should you do? (Clarify/Ask for more detail)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When a group has many ideas, what is the best way to make sure everyone agrees? (Summarize/Facilitate discussion)."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a "Club Promotion Video Project Brief," which includes a list of objectives, requirements, and a draft timeline. This document contains common instructions for club *activities* and *goals*, uses *imperatives* for directions (e.g., "Set goals," "Plan activities," "Record the video in the library," "Edit the footage"), and includes *simple present* descriptions (e.g., "The club meets in the library," "Our goal is to attract new *members*"). It also has sections for *feedback* and *suggestions*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will develop skills in clarifying written instructions in collaborative projects and facilitating discussions on project goals and tasks, especially for the "Club Promotion Video."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the "Project Brief" individually.
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main goal of this video project?" "What are the key *requirements* for the video?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match instructions from the brief (e.g., "Identify club strengths") with their purpose (e.g., "To highlight what makes the club unique").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Simple present for descriptions ("Our club *is* a great place...") and preferences ("I *prefer* to edit..."). Imperatives for instructions ("*Set* goals," "*Let's plan* activities," "*Coordinate* the filming").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> verbs: manage, negotiate, plan, organize, create, engage, discuss, respond, coordinate, assign, delegate, evaluate, contribute, participate, schedule, arrange, support, facilitate; nouns: project, event, community, feedback, suggestions, participation, leadership; adjectives: collaborative, responsible, supportive.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice stress on imperatives ("*Set* goals!") and on the third syllable from the end in verbs ending in -ate (e.g., "de-le-gate," "co-or-di-nate"). Focus on contrastive sounds /p/ in "participate" and /t/ in "team."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking about preferences ("What type of club would you like to join?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Negotiating roles and responsibilities, responding to feedback.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Clarifying Imperatives):</b> The teacher provides incomplete or vague instructions from the "Project Brief" (e.g., "Get equipment," "Talk about the club"). In pairs, students *mediate* by rephrasing these using full sentences and adding clarifying details, incorporating imperatives and target vocabulary. Example: Teacher writes: "Get equipment." Students say: "Let's *organize* and *assign* someone to find the necessary filming *equipment* by Friday."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Summarizing and Facilitating):</b> In small groups (3-4 students), the teacher gives each group a card with a scenario (e.g., "Your group has too many ideas for the video, and no one can agree on a main message."). Students must *facilitate* a mini-discussion, encouraging *participation*, and then summarize the key points and a potential *goal* using phrases like, "So far, we've agreed on..." or "Let's *coordinate* our efforts to focus on..." The teacher circulates, listening for correct use of simple present, imperatives, and target verbs like *discuss, coordinate, facilitate*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Negotiating Roles & Feedback):</b> Students imagine they are *club members* planning the "Club Promotion Video." They use the "Project Brief" to *negotiate* *roles* and *responsibilities* (e.g., "I would like to be the editor because I am good at video editing."). They practice giving and receiving *feedback* and *suggestions* on each other's proposed roles or video ideas. One student states a role, another responds with a clarifying question or suggestion: "You want to be the director? Can you *clarify* what your *responsibilities* would be?" or "That's a good *suggestion*, but I think we should *plan* to film *across from* the library for better lighting."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their established club groups (from previous lessons) to finalize the *planning* for their "Club Promotion Video." The task requires them to *mediate* the planning process.
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative project planning and presentation of a video outline. Each group must *facilitate* a discussion to *assign* specific *tasks* to each *member* for the video *project* (e.g., scriptwriting, filming, editing, presenting). They must clarify any ambiguous *instructions* from the "Project Brief" and *negotiate* *responsibilities* effectively. They will create a visual checklist or a step-by-step storyboard for their video, explicitly showing *who* will do *what* and *when*. For instance, "Step 1: Write script (Maria); Step 2: Film *in* the lab (Juan and Sofia); Step 3: Edit (Pedro)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in using mediation strategies (e.g., "Let's *clarify* that," "So, to *summarize*...", "What do you *suggest*?"), collaborative interaction, and successful completion of the video *project* outline. They must also *rephrase* instructions as needed and ensure all *members* understand their *roles*. They should use *simple past* to narrate a brief anecdote about their club's history or a positive experience from "Last year, I had a wonderful time with my friends at the club," which will be part of their video script. They will incorporate *prepositions of place* when describing potential filming locations (e.g., "We will film the interview *in* the school garden and a quick shot *across from* the main building").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a checklist focusing on mediation skills, combined with peer assessment during the group presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Club Promotion Video" outline or storyboard to another group or the whole class. During the presentation, students must *mediate* any questions from the audience, *clarify* their plan, and explain *how* they *negotiated roles* and *responsibilities*. They should be prepared to rephrase instructions or summarize their *goals* if there's confusion. They must clearly explain the use of *simple past* for anecdotes and *prepositions of place* for locations in their video concept.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation and rephrasing of instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective facilitation of group discussion and participation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., *clarify, summarize, coordinate, delegate, feedback, suggestions*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of *imperatives* and *simple present* for planning and *simple past* for anecdotes.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear *negotiation* of *roles* and *responsibilities*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of targeted sounds and word stress.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I help my group members understand the video project *tasks* and *goals* today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific way I *facilitated* our discussion or *clarified* an instruction?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of mediating discussions or clarifying instructions for our video *project*?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I improve my ability to *facilitate* communication and *clarify* complex ideas in future collaborative *projects*?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today's lesson focused on *mediation* skills for the "Club Promotion Video" *project*. Students engaged well in *negotiating roles* and *clarifying instructions*. The explicit modeling of rephrasing and summarizing was effective. Some groups struggled with proactively asking for *clarification*; I need to provide more sentence starters for initiating *mediation* in future lessons. The integration of *simple past* for anecdotes and *prepositions* for location was successful, especially when combined with the *project* visualization. I will ensure students have more opportunities to practice spontaneous *mediation* in diverse *scenarios*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "Learning to Manage a Club" and the "Club Promotion Video" project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, likely building on collaborative skills but with a new linguistic focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>